From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: new buffers zeroed

Windows' drivers give new video memory zeroed, and apps come to depend on it (patch 0026 zeroes new textures for
the same reason). 3DMark Vantage's GPU cloth creates its normals buffer without data and reads it in its first
frame, before its normals pass has written it: the host driver had left that buffer with whatever its memory held
(huge numbers and NaNs), the wind force made from those normals blew the cloth up, and every flag turned to NaN
within a frame (the test showed bare poles). New buffers are now cleared to zero (glClearBufferData). Sparse
buffers have no memory of their own. This also keeps one guest's buffer memory from showing another's leftovers.
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -8544,6 +8544,12 @@
    } else
       glBufferData(gr->target, width, NULL, GL_STREAM_DRAW);
 
+   /* Zeroed, as Windows' drivers give new video memory (as new textures are): apps read buffers they haven't written
+    * yet (3DMark Vantage's GPU cloth: last frame's normals, at its first frame), and the host driver leaves a new
+    * buffer with whatever its memory held (huge numbers and NaNs there: the cloth blew up) */
+   if (!vrend_state.use_gles && has_feature(feat_clear_texture))
+      glClearBufferData(gr->target, GL_R8, GL_RED, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, NULL);
+
    glBindBufferARB(gr->target, 0);
 }
 
